    "title": "Recycling at Our School",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Last year, our school had a big problem with rubbish. Every day, the students threw away hundreds of plastic bottles, paper cups and food. The cleaners filled twenty bags each week, and the school paid a lot of money to take the rubbish away."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "The head teacher, Mr Adams, asked the students for ideas. Some students suggested recycling. So in September the school bought coloured bins: blue for paper, green for glass, yellow for plastic and brown for food. A student group called the Green Team explained how to use them in a short video."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "At first, many students put their rubbish in the wrong bin, and the bins were very dirty. The Green Team put signs with pictures on each bin. They also gave a prize every month to the class with the best recycling. The winners got a free pizza lunch, so students became very interested."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "After six months, the school's rubbish fell by forty per cent. The money saved paid for new books in the library. The school also started a garden where students use the food waste to make soil for vegetables. Other schools in the town have now asked the Green Team for help, and they are planning a visit next month."
      }
    ]
  },
